CLAUDE.md Tips & Tricks for Product Designers
Nick Babich
Nick Babich

Following
5 min read
·
5 days ago
143


2





CLAUDE.md is a critical file for providing project-specific context to Claude Code. It serves as an onboarding guide for AI to your project requirements and codebase. Whenever Claude’s AI model writes new or modifies existing code in your project, the first thing it will do is check the CLAUDE.md file.

Here are 8 tips and tricks for writing CLAUDE.md that you need to remember:

1. Know the difference between project-based and global CLAUDE.md
We have two types of CLAUDE.md files:

Project-based
Located in the root folder of your project.
Global
Located at: ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md
Although both files share the same name, they serve different purposes.

A project-based CLAUDE.md is tightly coupled with a specific project.


A project-based CLAUDE.md for a landing page design project.
Think of project-based CLAUDE.md as project memory + execution guidelines.

For a web design project, this file can include:

Architecture decisions
Design system rules
Coding standards
Product-specific constraints
Business logic and domain knowledge
A global CLAUDE.md, on the other hand, defines persistent behaviors across all your projects. It’s ideal for:

Reusable workflows (e.g., “always create a changelog entry in the project”)
Personal preferences (tone, structure, output format)
Universal automation rules
Repetitive setup steps
Here is an example of instruction in the global CLAUDE.md file that asks Claude to create a timestamp when I started working on the project:

On project start:
- Create a .txt file in the project root directory
- File name format: YYYY-MM-DD_HH-NickBabich.txt 
(use current local date and hour)

File content (exact):
Project created by Nick Babich
2. Add essential sections to CLAUDE.md
A strong CLAUDE.md is not a list of random instructions; its a properly structured file.

Claude performs better when information is grouped semantically. Structured sections reduce ambiguity and improve retrieval.

Typical high-impact sections for product design project include:

Project Overview
Tech Stack
Folder Structure
Coding Guidelines
UI/UX Principles
Component Rules
Accessibility Requirements
Testing Strategy
Content/Copy Guidelines
Deployment Notes
I’ve dived into all the sections you should include CLAUDE.md in the following article:

CLAUDE.md Best Practices
10 Sections to Include in your CLAUDE.md
uxplanet.org

3. Use /init to generate a first draft of CLAUDE.md automatically
You don’t need to create CLAUDE.md from scratch. Instead, you can run a command in your project folder:

/init
and Claude Code will scan the project folder and draft a CLAUDE.md file based on the information it finds. This file it will generate won’t be perfect (the generated file is generic by design, and it will lack product intent and nuance), but it will help you avoid a blank-canvas state where you need to articulate all details yourself.

Press enter or click to view image in full size

You can run /init command even when you have an existing CLAUDE.md file. If you do that in Edit mode, Claude Code will update the file based on the recent information about the project.
4. Keep CLAUDE.md under 200 lines
Claude Code loads CLAUDE.md into every session it works on, and this consumes tokens. Thus, the shorter your CLAUDE.md is, the better because it leads to better adherence.

Press enter or click to view image in full size

Ideally, you should keep CLAUDE.md under 100 lines, but for many projects this is not possible because they have a lot of relevant details.
But what to do when you have a lot of relevant information about your project and want Claude to use this information when working on this project? If you put everything you have in CLAUDE.md file, it will be massive. We have an elegant solution for this problem and it’s called routing rules.

Routing rules is basically pointing Claude to other .md files you have in a project.

For design system documentation check @docs/design-system.md

For QA testing workflow check @workflows/qa-testing.md
This creates a modular knowledge system for Claude Code and AI will know that:

Core CLAUDE.md file → essential information about your project Claude needs to know.
Linked files → on-demand, detailed instructions that Claude will check when its working with a specific area (i.e., creates QA auto tests for your project).
5. Be specific when writing instructions
When you interact with humans and provide vague instructions, you likely get generic results. The same principle applies to AI, but the situation is even worse when you provide vague instructions to AI because Claude doesn’t “assume intent,” it follows patterns.

Thus, the more precise your instructions are, the more deterministic the output will be

For example, when you describe the requirement for coding UI components, its not enough to say:

"Write clean code"
Its much better to provide specific requirements such as:

- Keep components under 200 lines unless justified
- Use Tailwind utility classes (no inline styles)
- Prefer composition over inheritance
- Use descriptive prop names (no abbreviations)
💡 Here is a framework I use when writing instructions

Constraint (what AI should do)
Boundary (what limits AI should consider)
Example (what examples AI should use as a reference)
6. Treat CLAUDE.md as a living document
CLAUDE.md is not a file you create once and forget about it. It should evolve alongside your project.

Update it when:

You notice repeated mistakes that Claude makes when doing something
You introduce new patterns or tools and want Claude to use them
Product or business requirements were changed
Ideally, you should iterate the file on a daily basis by adding rules based on real usage, not theory.

7. Use .claude/rules to create modular rules
Instead of putting everything into one file, split instructions into topic-specific files. If you have specific rules on how to do things, add this information into topic-specific files:

.claude/rules/
  ├── ui.md
  ├── testing.md
  ├── accessibility.md
  ├── copywriting.md
You can then reference them via routing both in CLAUDE.md file:

@.claude/rules/ui.md
@.claude/rules/testing.md
and in Claude Code chat. In second case, you will create a perfect context for Claude Code as it will have information from CLAUDE.md file and reference to specific rules in a separate file:

Press enter or click to view image in full size

Input field for the Claude Code.
8. Commit CLAUDE.md to Git
Your project-based CLAUDE.md is part of your project’s infrastructure.

By committing it to Git, you will allow the entire team to get a similar AI behavior from Claude Code. What is even more important is that the knowledge becomes versioned and traceable.

