How to Prevent Claude Code from Creating Generic Web Designs (5 Proven Tips)
Nick Babich
Nick Babich

Following
8 min read
·
Mar 18, 2026
383


4





If you ask Claude to generate a web page for you, there is a high chance you will get a very generic output. For example, here is what you can get from Claude Code with a single prompt:

Code a landing page of a SaaS service called Roxy that offers
security solution for small business
Press enter or click to view image in full size

Landing page for a SaaS service generated by Claude Code. Pretty generic result. The page serves a functional purpose, but it’s not very appealing.
You can see that this design clearly serves the functional purpose, but doesn’t look very appealing.

But you can easily avoid that and create a more refined, sophisticated design that will be much harder to link to AI-generated stuff.

Here are 5 tips that will help you avoid that:

Start with CLAUDE.md file
The first thing you need to do when crafting a new web project is to create a CLAUDE.md file. CLAUDE.md will be the first file that Claude will access when working with your project. This file will create a context for Claude about your project. The file should cover everything Claude needs to know about the project, including project objectives and the technical stack you plan to use. You can read about the must-have sections for this file here:

CLAUDE.md Best Practices
10 Sections to Include in your CLAUDE.md
uxplanet.org

Here are essential tips for writing CLAUDE.md to get a better web design:

1. Provide instruction to use the ‘frontend-design’ skill
Claude has a built-in mechanism called Skills that help improve its abilities in a particular area. In a nutshell, a Skill is a set of custom instructions that help improve Claude’s performance for a particular task. When Claude is asked to perform a task, the first thing it will do is to check the Skills library to understand if it has a skill for this task.

Claude Skills for Product Designers
How to create your first Claude Skill
uxplanet.org

When it comes to web design, we want to get the most out of Claude in front-end coding. Thus, we want to have a Claude front-end design skill.

Good news is that Claude has a dedicated skill for front-end coding called ‘frontend-design.’ So it’s recommended to provide a clear instruction in the CLAUDE.md for Claude to invoke this skill every time it plans to do front-end coding

## DO THIS FIRST
**Invoke 'frontend-design' skill before generating any code.** Do it every
time you want to code front-end. In every session. Never skip this step.
Quick notes:

Make sure you have the ‘frontend-design’ skill installed in your Claude. You can do it by prompting Claude to show you the skills

show me what skills you have
Press enter or click to view image in full size

The output that Claude Code generated for me in response to the request to show the skills.
If you don’t see ‘frontend-design’ skill in the list Claude provides for you, run the following command, and it will be installed in the system:

/plugin install frontend-design@claude-code-plugins
This command will install frontend-design skill globally, meaning that you will be able to access it across all projects.

Know when to use ‘frontend-design’ skill provided by Claude and when to create your own front-end design skill. frontend-design skill provided by Claude will work fine in 95% of cases; you need to create your own skill for front-end design only when you have a very specific task you want Claude to handle when crafting design (such as you need to implement some unique attributes of visual identity in all web designs you create). But even in this case, you can handle this task by writing custom instructions in CLAUDE.md

2. Put visual references into a directory in the project and instruct Claude to use the references when crafting design
Having nice visual references during front-end design helps achieve much better results. You can add screenshots of real web pages you want to use as inspiration or you go to webistes like Behance and Dribbble and capture examples of nicely done design concepts.

Put the screenshots in a dedicated folder (in my case, I use a folder called ‘web_design_references’)

## Visual references

- The folder @web_design_references contains visual references I want
you to use when creating web pages
- If you see images in this folder: match layout structure, colors, spacing,
typography choices from the provided examples when coding our web page.
- If no reference images are provided: design from scratch, following the
rules provided below in the ‘Anti-Generic Design Guardrails’ section
- When you finish coding the page, capture a page screenshot of the page
you’ve generated and compare it against the references provided in the folder
@web_design_references. Find and fix any mismatches. Do at least two rounds of
comparison and fix. Keep all screenshots you capture in the folder @screenshots
Press enter or click to view image in full size

Folder ‘web_design_references’ contains visual inspiration for our future web page. This one in particular is from Dribble.
Quick note: Try to use no more than 2 visual references. Ideally, you should have only one reference for Claude Code, as it will make it much easier for AI to do a visual audit and refine the design according to the reference you provided.

3. Use a screenshot loop
Check the last part of this section, where we ask Claude to capture a screenshot of the page and compare it against the references provided.

- When you finish coding the page, capture page screenshot of the page
you've generated and compare it against references provided in the folder
@web_design_references. Find and fix any mismatches. Do at least two
rounds of comparison and fix. Keep all screenshots you capture in png format
in the the folder @screenshots
This technique is called screenshot loop, and it helps minimize the number of manual iterations that you need to go through to reach a desirable result. With this technique, you ask Claude to do the visual analysis and fix all issues along the way so the final result will look very close to the references you provide. And the last part of the prompt:

Keep all screenshots you capture in png format
in the folder @screeshots
Invite Claude to store all screenshots it took along the way in a separate folder in your project so you can check the process or refer them to Claude if you need to roll back to a certain version.

Press enter or click to view image in full size

‘Screenshot’ folder contains all screeshots that Claude captured during 2 rounds of refinement.
4. Fill out the ‘’Anti-Generic Design Guardrails’ section
And now let’s craft a section that prevents Claude from generating a generic design. It’s called Guardrails because it prevents Claude from doing something. The information in this section should be based on the tech stack you’re using. For my web projects, I typically rely on React + Tailwind CSS, so the section listed below contains cliches that we typically see in React/TailwindCSS designs.

## Anti-Generic Design Guardrails 

- **Colors:** Never use default Tailwind palette (`blue-500`, `indigo-600`,
 etc.). Always define and use custom design tokens from `tailwind.config`
 (e.g. `brand.primary`). Avoid raw hex values in JSX.

- **Typography:** Never use a single font (`font-sans`) everywhere. 
Pair fonts intentionally (`font-display` for headings, `font-body` for text).
Apply tight tracking (`tracking-[-0.03em]`) for headings and generous
line-height (`leading-[1.7]`) for body text.

- **Shadows:** Never use default utilities like `shadow-md` or `shadow-lg`.
 Use layered, color-tinted shadows defined in the theme.

- **Gradients & backgrounds:** Avoid flat fills (`bg-white`, `bg-gray-100`).
 Use layered gradients, overlays, or subtle textures.
Prefer custom gradients via arbitrary values instead of Tailwind presets.

- **Animations:** Never use `transition-all`. Only animate `transform`
and `opacity`. Use explicit utilities (`transition-transform`,
`transition-opacity`) and controlled durations. Prefer spring-like
motion when using animation libraries.

- **Component styling:** Avoid long, unstructured className strings
in JSX. Extract reusable components and variants. Use composition
patterns (`clsx`, `cva`) instead of duplicating styles.

- **Interactive states:** Every interactive element must include `hover`,
`focus-visible`, and `active` states. No exceptions.

- **Images:** Never use raw images without treatment. Always add overlays
(e.g. gradient), blending, or color tint to integrate them into the design.

- **Spacing & layout:** Avoid inconsistent spacing. Use a consistent
spacing scale (`px-6`, `py-12`, `gap-4`). Prefer grid-based layouts
over ad-hoc stacking.

- **Anti-patterns:** Avoid generic UI patterns like `bg-white + shadow-md +
rounded-lg`, default Tailwind look, missing states, or flat,
depth-less layouts.
Provide brand guidelines
Every brand has its own unique brand styling. So once you done with CLAUDE.md you need to add your brand assets to your project.

5. Create a dedicated directory for brand assets and ask Claude to use this information in design
Enrich the Claude context by adding all essential brand details, such as your brand logo, style guide (brand color palette, brand typography choices, icons, tone and voice guidelines, etc), that you want Claude in web design.

