Claude Code CLAUDE.md vs Skills
Nick Babich
Nick Babich

Following
5 min read
·
Mar 17, 2026
91


3





“What information should I put in CLAUDE.md, and what information should I put in Skills?” This is actually one of the most important architectural decisions when working with Claude Code at scale.

In this article, I want to go through both concepts and explain when and how to use them.

CLAUDE.md vs Skill
The way I think about CLAUDE.md and Skill is this

CLAUDE.md = “project brain”

Skills = “reusable capabilities”

📝 CLAUDE.md
Think of CLAUDE.md as “Everything Claude needs to know about this particular project.”

👉 If, when thinking about a particular detail, you start your thought with “In this project…” or “For this project…” then this information belongs in CLAUDE.md

Where CLAUDE.md is located
This file is typically located at the root of your project folder


Example of project structure (web project in React) with CLAUDE.md file in it
What goes into CLAUDE.md
The file itself is structured in markdown format (.md stands for markdown).

## Project Overview
Short explanation of what the project is.

## Tech Stack
- TypeScript
- Next.js
- Tailwind
- ShadCN

## Architecture
Explain folders and patterns.

## Coding Rules
- Use functional React components
- Prefer server components
- Use Tailwind utilities instead of custom CSS

## Design System
- Follow ShadCN patterns
- Use tokens from /styles/tokens.ts

## Commands
npm run dev
If you want to know which sections to include to your CLAUDE.md, read the following article:

CLAUDE.md Best Practices
10 Sections to Include in your CLAUDE.md
uxplanet.org

⚙️ Skills
Think of Skills as “Executable expertise Claude can apply on demand”

Skills are great for repeatable workflows like

Generate PRD
Create landing page
Refactor component
Design audit
Write API handler
Skills can benefit your project design (i.e., for a web design project, you might want to trigger skills that will craft your web design), but they don’t necessarily belong to a particular project (meaning that they can be used in all web design projects you’re working on)

Where Skills are located
Skills can be local and global. Local skills are a part of your project, and they are typically located in a /skills directory of the project folder:

project-root/
├── CLAUDE.md
├── skills/
│   ├── ux/
│   │   ├── run-ux-audit.md
│   │   └── analyze-user-flow.md
│   ├── coding/
│   │   ├── build-component.md
│   │   └── refactor-code.md
│   ├── research/
│   │   └── competitor-analysis.md
│   └── content/
│       └── write-linkedin-post.md
Local skills are skills that are used only in this particular project. If you want to use skill for other projects you work on, you should create Global skills. Global skills are located in Claude Code tool directory:

~/claude-skills/
├── front-end-design/
├── ux-audit/
└── content-writing/
Quick note: If you don’t know what Skills you have in your project, you can prompt Claude to show you available skills. The prompt can be this:

What skills I have 
Press enter or click to view image in full size

Claude Code output for this command on my local machine.
What goes into Skill
Just like CLAUDE.md, Skill is also a markdown file. Every skill needs a SKILL.md file with two parts:

Frontmatter in YAML (between --- markers). It tells Claude when to use the skill.
Markdown content with instructions Claude follows when the skill is invoked.
Below is an example of the skill I’ve created for landing page design:

name: create-landing-page
description: Generate a landing page using project conventions

steps:
  - Analyze CLAUDE.md for design system
  - Generate layout
  - Create components
  - Apply styling rules
  - Validate accessibility
If you’re interested in learning more about using Skills in the product design process, check the following article:

Claude Skills for Product Designers
How to create your first Claude Skill
uxplanet.org

🧩 How CLAUDE.md and Skills can work together
CLAUDE.md and Skills can be used together

CLAUDE.md can set foundational rules or integral constraints
Skill executes within those rules and constraints
For example, I can set specific versions of frameworks I use for my project in CLAUDE.md tech stack section:

## Tech Stack
- React version 19.2
- Use TypeScript 5.9.3
And I will also craft a reusable Skill build-component that will help me craft UI components for projects I work on:

Workflow:
1. Generate component in React
2. Use TypeScript types
3. Ensure <200 lines
4. Document component usage in markdown format
When the skill build-component will be triggered, it follow the workflow defined in this skill and honor the constraints I’ve defined in the CLAUDE.md

Quick note: You can also invoke or reference Skills in CLAUDE.mdfile. For example, here I provide quick shortcuts for two local skills I have in my project (run-ux-audit and build-component)

## Available Skills
- run-ux-audit → ./skills/ux/run-ux-audit.md
- build-component → ./skills/coding/build-component.md
3 Common Mistakes To Avoid When Crafting CLAUDE.md and Skills
❌ Putting workflows in CLAUDE.md
Try to avoid:

To create a landing page:
1. Do this
2. Do that
This should be either a Skill (if you’re planning to reuse it in future) or a dedicated workflow .md file that you can reference when asking Claude to build something.

❌ Putting project rules into Skills
Try to avoid adding details to Skill that are relevant to only one particular project (such as a specific version of a framework that your project uses):

Use Next.js 16.1.1
If you need to set constraints, do it in CLAUDE.md

❌ Duplicating logic
Don’t repeat rules you’ve already defined in CLAUDE.md inside Skills. Skills should read from CLAUDE.md, not redefine it.

