Current State of Claude Code and Figma Two-way Integration
Nick Babich
Nick Babich

Following
4 min read
·
Mar 5, 2026
187


2





Two-way Claude Code — Figma sync is a magical thing. You can prompt Claude to code your design, push it to Figma, refine it there, and then ask Claude to implement the changes you introduce in Figma! This makes the interactions so much easier.

However, this solution has a few important limitations. And it’s vital to clearly understand these limitations before you start using this tool in production. Based on my experience with this tool, I can identify 4 key limitations.

To illustrate individual issues, I will use the example of a landing page that Claude Code generated for me. You can check the entire process of web page coding, exporting to Figma and refining it in this article:

Claude Code + Figma = 💛
Did you know that Claude Code and Figma now support two-way communication? It means that you can publish a design from…
uxplanet.org

Press enter or click to view image in full size

Original design of a landing page that Claude Code generated for me.
1. Issue with auto layout
The great thing about exporting production code to Figma design is that design elements in Figma will have auto layout. But this auto layout is not perfect. There are many issues with auto layout that prevent turning UI elements into components right away. For example, in the exported version of the web page design, you can see that the simple UI element, a section “Trusted partners” that has auto-layout enabled, easily falls apart as soon as we change its size.

Press enter or click to view image in full size

As a result, most of the time you have to manually adjust the UI elements and only after that turn them into components.

If you’re building a responsive design (i.e., a landing page), it can easily fall apart as soon as you export it to Figma. Again, because individual components won’t be responsive when you start changing element sizes, the design will start to clash. For example, see what happens when I change individual elements on the page below. The entire frame, with design, is simply not responsive.

Press enter or click to view image in full size

2. Components are not applied
The second issue is relevant to the previous one. When you export product code from Claude to Figma, you get UI elements in a frame rather than UI components. For example, a collection of cards with testimonials in this design will be a grid of unattached UI elements, not a collection of instances of the same component.

Press enter or click to view image in full size

Card with a testimonial that we’ve exported from Claude Code to Figma. The card is not a component but an unattached element.
This happens even when you provide a link to design system to Claude Code.

3. Styling defects
Apart from spacing issues that happen every time,

Press enter or click to view image in full size

Spacing issue in the design exported to Figma.
another common issue is a style mismatch. For example, in the hero section of a landing page coded by Claude, you can see that the color of text in the hero section ‘ever happen’ has a nice gradient.

Press enter or click to view image in full size

Original design of a landing page that Claude Code generated for me.
But when we export it to Figma, the text will be pure white. I’ve noticed that this problem is more common for complex styles, such as the gradients mentioned above, but it can still occur in other cases.

Press enter or click to view image in full size

Missing text color in design exported to Figma from Claude.
4. Missed entire sections
This issue typically happens for complex designs. When you export a complex design from Claude, some parts may be lost during the export. In my example, you can see that some sections of the original coded design are missing in Figma.

Press enter or click to view image in full size

Figma design: Content in the section “Everything your security team needs” is missing.
And here is original coded design for the reference.

Press enter or click to view image in full size

Original web design coded by Claude Code.
Summary
The two-way connection between Figma and Claude Code has tremendous potential to become an essential tool in a designer’s toolkit. Even today, it’s great to have a tool that lets you modify design not by instructing Claude in plain words, but by making visual changes in the tool we’re all familiar with (Figma).

At the same time, the issues that I’ve listed above prevent the tool from being integrated into real workflows, so to me, two-way integration at its current state feels more like a beta feature rather than a final product.

