Claude Code + Figma = 💛
Nick Babich
Nick Babich

Following
7 min read
·
Mar 4, 2026
601


14





Did you know that Claude Code and Figma now support two-way communication? It means that you can publish a design from Claude to Figma, make changes in Figma, and apply changes to the coded version of your design.

In this article, I will walk you through the entire 4-step process for connecting Claude Code to Figma and using this connection to streamline the design process.

Step 1: Setup
The first thing we need to do is to set up Figma MCP in Claude Code. MCP is a universal interface for AI models that enables them to read files, execute functions, and handle contextual operations in 3rd-party services. In our case, Claude Code will access Figma via MCP.

I will use Claude Code in a terminal. Launch a terminal on your Mac or PC, and type:

claude
Next, you will need to set up the MCP connection. I will use a remote MCP server connection (connects directly to Figma’s hosted endpoint). To configure it, you need to type the command:

claude mcp add --transport http figma https://mcp.figma.com/mcp
Press enter or click to view image in full size

Adding Figma MCP to the list of MCPs that Claude can use.
Once Claude has configured the server, it’s recommended to restart the Claude session to maximise the chances that everything will work fine. So I will type a command

/exit
Press enter or click to view image in full size

Restarting Claude Code session.
And after that, create a new session in the same terminal window by sending a command.

claude
The first thing I need to do in the new session is configure the MCP server. To do it I will type a command.

/mcp
Press enter or click to view image in full size

List of MCPs that Claude Code has.
Claude will show me a list of MCPs I have, and I will see the Figma MCP on that list. I need to choose it from the list and configure it. The process of configuration is straightforward: you need to authenticate your Figma account so MCP will have access to the right Figma space. Choose Authenticate from the list.

Press enter or click to view image in full size

Authenticating Figma MCP.
And Claude will open a browser window with a request for authentication. You should expect to see a window like this.

❗Important: If you have multiple Figma accounts, make sure that you provide access to the right account. Check email in this window and click “Switch accounts” if you see that the wrong account was used. If you choose the wrong Figma account, Claude won’t be able to access your Figma files, and you will need to start the process over again.

Press enter or click to view image in full size

Providing access to Figma account for Claude.
After you allow the access, you should see a confirmation message from Claude: “Authentication successful.”

Press enter or click to view image in full size

Figma authentication successful.
But there is one thing we need to do before starting to use the Figma and Claude connection. Install plugins that Claude will use to access Figma and make contextual operations. To do that, you need to type a command.

/plugin install figma@claude-plugins-official
Press enter or click to view image in full size

Installing Figma plugins for Claude Code.
The plugins will enable two-way, AI-powered design-to-code workflows and allow you to generate editable Figma frames from code and vice versa.

Press enter or click to view image in full size

List of plugins that Claude Code has.
Step 2: Asking Claude to code design
Now we can start using our Claude and Figma integration. And the first thing that I will do is code a landing page for a SaaS service. The prompt I will use is this:

code a landing page for SaaS service called Roxy (security solution) 
https://wwww.figma/com/design/[LINK]
Press enter or click to view image in full size

Coding a first version of our web page design.
You can notice that I’ve added a link to the Figma page. Right now, I don’t have anything in this Figma file, and I’ll use this link in the future (when I transfer a coded design to Figma).

Press enter or click to view image in full size

Empty Figma file that we will populate with our design.
Claude will try to reach the Figma file for design (it assumes it should transfer the existing design from Figma to code), but when it doesn’t find a design, it will show us an error message (“You currently have nothing selected”). We can simply ignore this error message right now because we know we have nothing in Figma at this point.

Press enter or click to view image in full size

Claude tells us that Figma link is empty. We know that :)
It will take Claude a few minutes to create a landing page for our SaaS. And here is a preview of this page in a browser (Claude will show the preview automatically).

Press enter or click to view image in full size

First design that Claude Code generated for us.
Step 3: Asking Claude to transfer the design to Figma
After we get the coded design, we can prompt Claude to send it to Figma.

transfer this design into Figma
Press enter or click to view image in full size

Asking Claude Code to send this design to Figma.
And after a short wait time, our Figma file will be populated with a real web page design.

❗Important : It’s vital to mention that the design that we got in Figma is not perfect. It has many spacing issues, and some elements of the original page are not visible in Figma. We can fix the issues by writing follow-up prompts to Claude.

